from pathlib import Path
import json,hashlib
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
pins={x['head'][:12]:x for n in [1,2] for x in json.loads((R/f'source-pins-batch{n}.json').read_text())}
# Each answer is a source-grounded retrospective review, never an invented agent trace.
CASES=[
('6281442d97bd','diagnose','correctness','A fused add+norm cache sees [1,16,D] verification and [16,1,D] batched decode. Why can equal row counts still be an invalid cache key? State the minimum repair and the needed regression scope.', 'The cached configuration carries output layout, not merely the number of rows. Reusing the verification configuration for a batched tick can return the wrong shape despite identical element counts. Key it by the complete batch/sequence layout and preserve each caller’s output layout. The source reports that this mis-layout caused the GDN step to decline and a fallback concat to fail every slot. Guard equal-row-count/different-layout calls, more than one block of concurrent streams, and single-row ownership after a member leaves a persistent group. The reported 32-stream completion and189 tok/s are historical author observations on an M5 Ultra, not a benchmark reproduced here.'),
('dce06d208cbc','review_correctness','correctness','A uint4 KV staging kernel accepts aligned strides. A BF16 [1,H,T,264] view sliced to [3,259) fails, but [4,260) appears correct. Is checking row/head strides sufficient? Explain the evidence gap for lazy arrays.', 'No. The view’s bound base can be offset independently of its strides; the first slice starts6 bytes into the storage and violates the16-byte uint4 alignment contract. A single apparently working offset is not a sound alignment test. The source change declines an evaluated K or V with a misaligned bound address and preserves the existing fallback; its regression includes a failing offset plus an aligned twin. However, its helper returns true for unevaluated arrays because no address is available. Thus the captured fix and tests establish the reported evaluated-view cases, not every lazy-view case. A stronger follow-up should check the actual bound pointer in the kernel or otherwise prove/evaluate alignment before launch. That follow-up is a recommendation, not an executed result.'),
('080a62e2f2d7','select_dispatch','neutral','Select a conservative causal attention split policy from the historical vector-kernel limits and preferred fused NAX path. Explain why shape parity alone did not justify enabling every head dimension.', 'The source gives two vector limits: at most8 query rows and qL*gqa <=32. Split into min(8,32/gqa) rows, retaining each group’s exact causal key endpoint; for GQA12 this means groups of2, not the old GQA6 assumption of5. The split must decline when the caller’s preferred force-fused NAX path would serve the request, or a fallback optimization preempts a better arm. The final default-on policy was restricted to head dimension256 because that architecture had been timed; other dimensions remained declined despite parity coverage. Unit parity, dispatch selection, and measured architecture eligibility are separate gates. This message documents source-reported tests and policy; it does not supply a new independent timing result.'),
('9a5467375008','diagnose','neutral','An attention kernel reduces dispatches, yet enabling it initially gives unchanged live speed. Use the captured routing and view-alignment findings to explain what must be checked before attributing a speedup.', 'A working kernel is insufficient if production routing never reaches it. The source reports that selector floors inherited from union gather kept dense verification on the mask path, producing unchanged text and speed with the new arm toggled. Repair selector, attention caller, and memory-admission predicates together and test the production route. Separately, stride-only alignment gates missed offset/lazy views: the final dense kernel checks the actual bound pointer and strides, falling back to element loads. Pinning the quantized kernel source protects the unrelated variant during refactoring. The60 versus141 operations across12 layers are author-reported dispatch counts, not proof of proportional elapsed-time improvement. Confirm numerical correctness and run controlled end-to-end A/B measurements on the engaged path.'),
('3dc1dab54e7b','review_correctness','correctness','Review a1024-thread GDN recurrence/norm-gate/rollback fusion. What contracts and fallback behavior are needed beyond output parity on the development GPU?', 'Preserve all outputs of the original chain, including rollback captures and stored-width rounding, rather than checking only the activation. Validate every indexed shape and state/weight extent: the source describes a malformed z width that silently shifted the next row’s gate. A1024-thread layout is a per-pipeline, per-device capability requirement, not a universal Metal limit; the first evaluated dispatch may refuse it. Probe with a clean GPU stream, recognize only the specific thread-limit refusal, keep unrelated errors visible, and decline to the original chain. Mark a width usable only after a clean evaluated probe. The source reports parity and these regression repairs; no local test or timing was run for this library.'),
('987c40030406','reject_optimization','tradeoff','The author reports a denser down kernel18% faster in isolation, no end-to-end gain, and rejected48/64-row GEMM windows. What should an optimization agent conclude, including the toolchain and numerical limits of the accepted changes?', 'Do not promote the isolated18% figure as a model speedup. The source explicitly says that the denser down kernel won nothing end to end and that larger windows hit a register cliff. Keep those as failed or nontransferable attempts. The accepted bundle also fixed a macOS27 NAX template address-space error and moved the optional-kernel probe to evaluated compilation, so fallback can actually catch rejection. Its controlled M5 Max forward/prefill gains belong to the bundle and stated workload, not every individual edit. Its changed reduction tree matched greedy output for43 tokens and then diverged near a small top-two gap; this is not universal bitwise equivalence. Separate toolchain repair, resource tradeoffs, numerical acceptance and end-to-end measurement.'),
('6755ff2426b7','explain_change','positive','A quantized KV cache is dequantized into a whole-layer BF16 buffer even though attention already splits keys into windows. Explain the memory optimization and the coordinate invariant that makes it safe.', 'Separate where the temporary buffer begins from the absolute cache coordinates used for causal, band and sparse-attention masking. The source adds a buffer offset and absolute cache length, while staging remains relative to the dispatch-local buffer. Dequantize only the bounded packed-key slice for each dispatch and carry the existing FP32 online-softmax state unchanged; dense callers retain their original route. The reported admission reductions are memory accounting results, not token/s measurements. The source claims byte equality over the same dequantized values for the tested sliced/dense chains, which must not be extended to an unquantized truth claim. Test absolute-coordinate boundaries, partial windows, carries, the dense fallback and matching admission estimates.'),
('b89991a4e6ab','select_dispatch','tradeoff','Choose between split-K packed attention, direct packed gather and a single dense rebuild. The captured microbench shows a query-width crossover and repeated row staging. Why is one universally packed route inappropriate?', 'At small query counts the split-K route reuses the decode arithmetic efficiently. The source extends that arm only to40 rows, then chooses packed gather while S*2048 <=4*kv bounds repeated staging to four times per row. Beyond that reuse budget, one dense rebuild plus gather can be cheaper; the report includes packed-gather regressions at larger widths. Keep memory accounting and the kill switch consistent with the selected route. Treat the crossover as workload/device-specific and retain parity against the declared dequantized reference and tolerance. The source also records near-tie generation divergence, so successful tolerance tests do not justify a universal byte-identical-output claim.'),
('2d58ab608455','reject_optimization','positive','Every verification row could share a single large attention group, but the source reports it17–31% slower at3–4 rows despite exact output. What narrower grouping was accepted and which dispatch condition preserves the row contract?', 'The accepted layout groups rows in pairs, with a final group of three, to share each packed K/V page walk without the larger resource cost of grouping all rows. Each row retains the decode kernel’s matmul, softmax and rescale order. Engage only where all rows share the last row’s split partition and the serial decode template has passed its probe; any extra page for a shorter row must be fully masked and contribute exact zero. Otherwise preserve one dispatch per row. The reported5–7% verify-forward gain and17–31% rejected-arm loss are author measurements for the stated long-cache workload, not local results or a guarantee for other geometries.'),
('c2600a40739a','interpret_benchmark','neutral','A KDA fusion removes up to68 dispatches per token and matches captured outputs bit for bit. Decode changes28.24 to28.18 ms/token. Should the training target call this an established speedup?', 'No. The author explicitly calls the0.2% timing difference inside noise. Dispatch removal and exact-output evidence support the mechanism and correctness scope, but do not establish a practically resolved elapsed-time gain. Preserve the reported dense-BF16 eligibility, original GEMV accumulation order and fallback for affine, FP8 or covariance-tap inputs. Record that the owner chose to land it, rather than converting that decision into a benchmark result. Further timing would need controlled paired repetitions with a predeclared noise/benefit criterion; none was executed for this library.'),
('3adb8002840a','interpret_benchmark','tradeoff','Interpret a GDN FP32-state change reported to cost about2% aggregate throughput and54MB per persisted checkpoint, with KLD gain inside noise. What must accompany the precision decision?', 'Describe it as an adopted precision/storage tradeoff, not a measured speed or quality win. The source uses FP32 state beside the FP32 decay gate and removes the former user switch. Persisted state needs a distinct namespace so old BF16 entries are not restored under the new numerical contract, and admission/checkpoint accounting must charge four bytes per state element. The quoted throughput/memory costs and unresolved KLD gain remain author reports. Do not use the intuition about small decay rounding as proof of downstream quality improvement; evaluate that separately under a matched teacher and declared task distribution.'),
('846428239de2','diagnose','correctness','A CPU double-precision reference confirms an attention matrix formula, yet a native-BF16 fixture changes routing near a four-row prefill tail. Why can both observations be true, and how should validation be scoped?', 'The formula can be correct while intermediate precision changes perturb nearly tied downstream MoE routing scores. The source attributes the fixture to half-query rounding and keeps tails of at most8 rows on FP32-query decode-style arithmetic, leaving large-prefill kernels unchanged. Extend kernel and full-logit coverage through that tail boundary; a stand-alone attention oracle alone does not exercise route sensitivity. This is the author’s diagnosed case and bounded repair, not evidence that all BF16 attention is incorrect or that every downstream route is preserved. No model was run by this library.'),
('582d809fd300','plan_validation','correctness','A GLM decode repair changes an all-selected-rows-valid promise to a guaranteed valid prefix because pooled selections contain0xffffffff padding. The author ran only make and git diff --check. What can be claimed and what checks remain?', 'The recorded validation supports a successful build and clean patch formatting on the stated M3 Ultra; it does not establish GPU parity or performance. The repair must route padded selections through the bounds-checked kernel while retaining the all-valid fast path only for genuinely contiguous/top-k producers. Validate live rows, padded sentinel slots, causal-tail lengths, cache bounds and cross-layer reuse of the selected-row metadata; compare actual attention outputs and full logits where routing is sensitive. Time the valid-prefix and fallback paths separately if claiming speed. These are proposed follow-up checks, not invented observations.'),
('b64d3e0689a6','review_correctness','positive','Review a compressor-finalization fusion that runs multiple virtual-width phases inside one Metal threadgroup. What is the central barrier hazard, and what numerical/source-evidence boundaries should remain visible?', 'Threads outside an individual phase’s virtual width must still reach every threadgroup barrier. Predicate phase arithmetic and stores, not barrier participation. Preserve each original phase’s reduction topology, thread counts, rounding and state-shift indexing; copying algebra alone is insufficient for bitwise parity. The source reports an eleven-to-one dispatch consolidation on emit tokens and six controlled M5 Max inverse A/B gains around0.4–0.5%, with exact retained-block comparisons. Those are historical author receipts summarized in a commit message, not locally reproduced measurements or a guarantee on other devices. Preserve the opt-in/rollback dispatch policy and test emit/non-emit boundaries.'),
('540e6ae59a58','review_correctness','correctness','A one-pass gate/up kernel lets lane l stage column l in32-input steps, but host and kernel independently use a literal32-thread count. What invariant should be made explicit before future tuning?', 'Make the staged tile’s32-column and32-input-step requirements explicit beside its layout, and share the named thread-count constant between the host dispatch and kernel table fill. The mapping is only valid under that geometry; silently changing one side can leave columns unstaged or interpret the wrong data even if the code compiles. Add assertion and dispatch-contract tests before varying tile geometry. The captured change establishes a source-level guard and shared ABI constant; its commit supplies no measured speed gain, and this library executed no kernel.'),
('9402155ae6ed','interpret_benchmark','correctness','The same Q4 metallib behaves differently with shader validation on macOS26.5/M5 Pro, yet works on macOS27/M5 Max. An always-inline fix has timing geomean0.9998 with2% spread. Classify the result and its limits.', 'Classify it as a correctness/toolchain compatibility repair with neutral measured performance. The source reports that out-of-line q4_prefill calls forwarding threadgroup storage produced wrong outputs under shader validation on the older toolchain/device combination, while forcing inlining restored the intended kernel shape. Testing only the newer environment or validation-disabled path would miss it. Preserve coverage across both reported environments and the failing output-sums variants. A0.9998 ratio is unresolved within2% spread, not a credible speedup. These tests and timings are upstream author reports; the library has not recompiled or executed the pair.')]
rows=[]
for short,task,outcome,question,answer in CASES:
 pin=pins[short];repo=pin['repository'];case=repo+'-'+short+'-hour-v3';p=R/'sourcepacks'/case/'commit-message.txt';raw=p.read_bytes();body=raw.decode();lic=[]
 for l in pin['licenses']:lic.append({'path':str(R/l['path']),'sha256':l['sha256']})
 row={'id':case+'-reasoning','repo':pin['url'],'commit':pin['head'],'parent':pin['parent'],'lineage':'mlx-sushi-connected-training'if repo in['sushi','mlx-serve']else repo+'-related-optimization-training','task_type':task,'source_evidence':[{'path':str(p),'sha256':hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest(),'url':pin['url']+'/commit/'+pin['head'],'quote':body}], 'prompt':'You are reviewing a historical Metal optimization record. The following is author-reported evidence, not a tool observation from this conversation.\n\n'+body+'\n\nReview task: '+question,'completion':answer,'rationale_basis':'mixed','outcome_class':outcome,'review':{'status':'approved','reviewer':'Codex quality_research source-grounded review 2026-10-13','evidence_fidelity':True,'no_fabricated_observations':True,'task_answer_separation':True,'lineage_screened':True},'protection_review':{'status':'cleared','reason':'Exact captured public commit/parent screened against protected identity ledger; related upstream engine revisions remain grouped. This is retrospective evidence interpretation, not an independent held-out optimization task.'},'validation':{'local_execution':'not_run'},'licenses':lic}
 rows.append(row)
(R/'reasoning-records.json').write_text(json.dumps({'format':'historical-metal-evidence-reasoning-v1','records':rows},indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
print(len(rows),'reasoning records')
